</style>
</head>
<body>

<div class="header">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Amazon Bestselling Books: Price vs. User Rating Trend Analysis</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">A decade of bestseller data (2009–2019) — examining the relationship between book pricing and customer satisfaction across Fiction and Non-Fiction genres.</div>
    <div class="meta">Data Source: Amazon Top 50 Bestsellers (2009–2019) &bull; {total} records &bull; {unique_books} unique titles &bull; Report generated April 2026</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="container">

<!-- KPI Strip -->
<div class="kpi-strip">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{avg_rating:.2f}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg User Rating</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">${avg_price:.2f}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg Book Price</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card accent">
    <div class="kpi-value">{pearson_r:.4f}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Price-Rating Correlation</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card green">
    <div class="kpi-value">{avg_reviews:,.0f}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg Review Count</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-value">{total}</div>
    <div class="kpi-label">Total Records</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Section 1: Market Overview -->
<section id="market-overview">
  <h2>1. Bestseller Market Overview (2009–2019)</h2>
  <p>This analysis covers <strong>{total} bestseller entries</strong> representing <strong>{unique_books} unique titles</strong> across 11 years of Amazon's top-50 annual lists. Books can appear in multiple years — a sign of enduring market appeal.</p>

  <div class="comparison-grid">
    <div class="genre-card fiction">
      <h4 style="color: var(--fiction);">Fiction ({fiction_count} entries, {fiction_count*100/total:.1f}%)</h4>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Average Price</span><span class="stat-value">${genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_price']:.2f}</span></div>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Average Rating</span><span class="stat-value">{genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_rating']:.2f}</span></div>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Avg Reviews</span><span class="stat-value">{genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_reviews']:,.0f}</span></div>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Price Range</span><span class="stat-value">${genre_stats['Fiction']['min_price']}–${genre_stats['Fiction']['max_price']}</span></div>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Rating Range</span><span class="stat-value">{genre_stats['Fiction']['min_rating']:.1f}–{genre_stats['Fiction']['max_rating']:.1f}</span></div>
    </div>
    <div class="genre-card nonfiction">
      <h4 style="color: var(--nonfiction);">Non-Fiction ({nonfiction_count} entries, {nonfiction_count*100/total:.1f}%)</h4>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Average Price</span><span class="stat-value">${genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_price']:.2f}</span></div>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Average Rating</span><span class="stat-value">{genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_rating']:.2f}</span></div>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Avg Reviews</span><span class="stat-value">{genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_reviews']:,.0f}</span></div>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Price Range</span><span class="stat-value">${genre_stats['Non Fiction']['min_price']}–${genre_stats['Non Fiction']['max_price']}</span></div>
      <div class="stat-row"><span class="stat-label">Rating Range</span><span class="stat-value">{genre_stats['Non Fiction']['min_rating']:.1f}–{genre_stats['Non Fiction']['max_rating']:.1f}</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:350px;">
    <canvas id="genreSplitChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Non-Fiction commands a <strong>${genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_price'] - genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_price']:.2f} premium</strong> in average price over Fiction, yet Fiction achieves a slightly {'higher' if genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_rating'] > genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_rating'] else 'lower'} average rating ({genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_rating']:.2f} vs. {genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_rating']:.2f}). Fiction also generates more reviews on average ({genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_reviews']:,.0f} vs. {genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_reviews']:,.0f}), suggesting stronger reader engagement per title.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 2: Price-Rating Correlation -->
<section id="price-rating-correlation">
  <h2>2. Price vs. User Rating: Correlation Analysis</h2>
  <p>The Pearson correlation coefficient between book price and user rating across all {total} records is <strong>r = {pearson_r:.4f}</strong>. This indicates a <strong>{'weak negative' if pearson_r < -0.1 else 'very weak negative' if pearson_r < 0 else 'very weak positive' if pearson_r < 0.1 else 'weak positive'} correlation</strong> — {'higher-priced books tend to receive marginally lower ratings' if pearson_r < 0 else 'price and rating show virtually no linear relationship'}.</p>

  <h3>Scatter Plot: Price vs. User Rating by Genre</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="scatterChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Correlation by Genre</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Genre</th><th>Pearson r</th><th>Interpretation</th><th>Avg Price</th><th>Avg Rating</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Fiction</td><td>{genre_pearson['Fiction']:.4f}</td><td>{'Weak negative' if genre_pearson['Fiction'] < -0.1 else 'Very weak negative' if genre_pearson['Fiction'] < 0 else 'Very weak positive' if genre_pearson['Fiction'] < 0.1 else 'Weak positive'}</td><td>${genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_price']:.2f}</td><td>{genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_rating']:.2f}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Non-Fiction</td><td>{genre_pearson['Non Fiction']:.4f}</td><td>{'Weak negative' if genre_pearson['Non Fiction'] < -0.1 else 'Very weak negative' if genre_pearson['Non Fiction'] < 0 else 'Very weak positive' if genre_pearson['Non Fiction'] < 0.1 else 'Weak positive'}</td><td>${genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_price']:.2f}</td><td>{genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_rating']:.2f}</td></tr>
      <tr style="font-weight:600;"><td>Overall</td><td>{pearson_r:.4f}</td><td>{'Weak negative' if pearson_r < -0.1 else 'Very weak negative' if pearson_r < 0 else 'Very weak positive' if pearson_r < 0.1 else 'Weak positive'}</td><td>${avg_price:.2f}</td><td>{avg_rating:.2f}</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Average Rating by Price Segment</h3>
  <div class="chart-container" style="max-height:350px;">
    <canvas id="segmentChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Price Segment</th><th>Book Count</th><th>% of Total</th><th>Avg Rating</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
"""

for seg in seg_labels:
    cnt = segment_data[seg]['count']
    ar = sum(segment_data[seg]['ratings']) / len(segment_data[seg]['ratings'])
    pct = cnt * 100 / total
    html += f'      <tr><td>{seg}</td><td>{cnt}</td><td>{pct:.1f}%</td><td>{ar:.2f}</td></tr>\n'

html += f"""    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Price has minimal impact on customer satisfaction among bestsellers. The near-zero correlation (r = {pearson_r:.4f}) confirms that <strong>pricing and quality perception are largely independent</strong> in this segment. The lowest-priced books ($0–$5) and the highest-priced books ($31+) show broadly similar ratings, indicating that perceived value is driven by content quality, not price point.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 3: Genre Comparison -->
<section id="genre-comparison">
  <h2>3. Fiction vs. Non-Fiction: Pricing and Rating Strategies</h2>

  <h3>Side-by-Side Comparison</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Metric</th><th style="color:#93c5fd;">Fiction</th><th style="color:#fca5a5;">Non-Fiction</th><th>Difference</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>Entries</td><td>{fiction_count}</td><td>{nonfiction_count}</td><td>{fiction_count - nonfiction_count:+d}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Avg Price</td><td>${genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_price']:.2f}</td><td>${genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_price']:.2f}</td><td>${genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_price'] - genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_price']:+.2f}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Avg Rating</td><td>{genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_rating']:.2f}</td><td>{genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_rating']:.2f}</td><td>{genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_rating'] - genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_rating']:+.2f}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Avg Reviews</td><td>{genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_reviews']:,.0f}</td><td>{genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_reviews']:,.0f}</td><td>{genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_reviews'] - genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_reviews']:+,.0f}</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Price Range</td><td>${genre_stats['Fiction']['min_price']}–${genre_stats['Fiction']['max_price']}</td><td>${genre_stats['Non Fiction']['min_price']}–${genre_stats['Non Fiction']['max_price']}</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Rating Range</td><td>{genre_stats['Fiction']['min_rating']:.1f}–{genre_stats['Fiction']['max_rating']:.1f}</td><td>{genre_stats['Non Fiction']['min_rating']:.1f}–{genre_stats['Non Fiction']['max_rating']:.1f}</td><td>—</td></tr>
      <tr><td>Pearson r (Price-Rating)</td><td>{genre_pearson['Fiction']:.4f}</td><td>{genre_pearson['Non Fiction']:.4f}</td><td>—</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Yearly Average Price by Genre</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="genrePriceChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Yearly Average Rating by Genre</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="genreRatingChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> Non-Fiction consistently commands higher prices but does not achieve higher ratings. Fiction delivers <strong>better perceived value</strong> — lower prices with comparable or slightly higher satisfaction scores. Non-Fiction's wider price range (driven by reference and academic titles) introduces more pricing variability without corresponding quality gains.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 4: Temporal Trends -->
<section id="temporal-trends">
  <h2>4. Year-over-Year Trends in Pricing and Ratings</h2>
  <p>This section tracks annual shifts in pricing, ratings, and review volumes across the decade — the most critical section for identifying inflection points in market dynamics.</p>

  <h3>Average Price & Rating Trend (2009–2019)</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="dualAxisChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Annual Summary Table</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Year</th><th>Avg Price</th><th>YoY Price Change</th><th>Avg Rating</th><th>YoY Rating Change</th><th>Avg Reviews</th><th>Fiction / Non-Fiction</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
"""

for i, y in enumerate(years):
    pc = f"{price_changes[i-1]:+.2f}" if i > 0 else "—"
    rc = f"{rating_changes[i-1]:+.3f}" if i > 0 else "—"
    fc = yearly_genre[y].get('Fiction', {}).get('count', 0)
    nc = yearly_genre[y].get('Non Fiction', {}).get('count', 0)
    html += f'      <tr><td><strong>{y}</strong></td><td>${yearly[y]["avg_price"]:.2f}</td><td>{pc}</td><td>{yearly[y]["avg_rating"]:.2f}</td><td>{rc}</td><td>{yearly[y]["avg_reviews"]:,.0f}</td><td>{fc} / {nc}</td></tr>\n'

html += f"""    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Average Review Volume Trend</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="reviewTrendChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Genre Mix Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-container" style="max-height: 350px;">
    <canvas id="genreMixChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Inflection points identified:</strong>
    <ul style="margin:8px 0 0 18px;">
      <li><strong>{max_price_year}:</strong> Peak average price at ${yearly[max_price_year]['avg_price']:.2f} — the most expensive year for bestsellers.</li>
      <li><strong>{min_price_year}:</strong> Lowest average price at ${yearly[min_price_year]['avg_price']:.2f} — reflects a market shift toward affordability.</li>
      <li><strong>{max_rating_year}:</strong> Highest average rating ({yearly[max_rating_year]['avg_rating']:.2f}) — customer satisfaction peaked here.</li>
      <li><strong>{min_rating_year}:</strong> Lowest average rating ({yearly[min_rating_year]['avg_rating']:.2f}) — the weakest year for reader sentiment.</li>
      <li><strong>{years[biggest_price_drop_idx+1]}:</strong> Largest year-over-year price drop (${price_changes[biggest_price_drop_idx]:+.2f}).</li>
      <li><strong>{years[biggest_price_rise_idx+1]}:</strong> Largest year-over-year price increase (${price_changes[biggest_price_rise_idx]:+.2f}).</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 5: Top Authors -->
<section id="top-authors">
  <h2>5. Dominant Authors on the Bestseller List</h2>
  <p>Authors who repeatedly appear on the annual top-50 list demonstrate sustained market relevance. The table below ranks the top 15 by total appearances across the decade.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Rank</th><th>Author</th><th>Appearances</th><th>Avg Price</th><th>Avg Rating</th><th>Price Range</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
"""

for rank, (author, freq) in enumerate(top_authors, 1):
    ap = sum(author_prices[author]) / len(author_prices[author])
    ar = sum(author_ratings[author]) / len(author_ratings[author])
    minp = min(author_prices[author])
    maxp = max(author_prices[author])
    html += f'      <tr><td>{rank}</td><td>{author}</td><td>{freq}</td><td>${ap:.2f}</td><td>{ar:.2f}</td><td>${minp}–${maxp}</td></tr>\n'

html += f"""    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="authorChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="insight-box">
    <strong>Key takeaway:</strong> The most frequently appearing authors maintain <strong>high ratings (typically 4.6+)</strong> with <strong>moderate pricing</strong>. Sustained bestseller status correlates with consistent quality delivery at accessible price points. Prolific authors like {top_authors[0][0]} ({top_authors[0][1]} appearances) and {top_authors[1][0]} ({top_authors[1][1]} appearances) demonstrate that building a perennial presence on the list is achievable through proven reader satisfaction rather than premium pricing.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- Section 6: Strategic Recommendations -->
<section id="recommendations">
  <h2>6. Strategic Recommendations</h2>
  <p>The following recommendations are grounded in the data patterns identified in sections 1–5. Each traces back to a specific finding.</p>

  <ol class="rec-list">
    <li>
      <strong>Price for accessibility, not prestige.</strong> The near-zero price-rating correlation (r = {pearson_r:.4f}) demonstrates that premium pricing does not improve customer satisfaction. The majority of bestsellers fall in the $5–$15 range. <em>Recommendation:</em> Target the ${seg_labels[1] if len(seg_labels) > 1 else '$6–$10'}–{seg_labels[3] if len(seg_labels) > 3 else '$11–$15'} range for maximum market penetration without sacrificing perceived quality.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Differentiate pricing strategy by genre.</strong> Fiction bestsellers average ${genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_price']:.2f} vs. Non-Fiction at ${genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_price']:.2f}, yet Fiction achieves {'higher' if genre_stats['Fiction']['avg_rating'] > genre_stats['Non Fiction']['avg_rating'] else 'comparable'} ratings. <em>Recommendation:</em> Keep Fiction pricing aggressive to maximize volume; for Non-Fiction, justify higher prices with perceived utility value (reference, self-help, cookbooks).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Invest in review generation.</strong> Average review counts have {'grown' if yearly[2019]['avg_reviews'] > yearly[2009]['avg_reviews'] else 'fluctuated'} over the decade (from {yearly[2009]['avg_reviews']:,.0f} in 2009 to {yearly[2019]['avg_reviews']:,.0f} in 2019). High review volumes correlate with sustained bestseller presence. <em>Recommendation:</em> Prioritize post-purchase review solicitation campaigns, especially for Fiction titles that already show strong engagement.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Monitor the downward price trend.</strong> Average bestseller price {'declined' if yearly[2019]['avg_price'] < yearly[2009]['avg_price'] else 'shifted'} from ${yearly[2009]['avg_price']:.2f} in 2009 to ${yearly[2019]['avg_price']:.2f} in 2019. <em>Recommendation:</em> Align new release pricing with the current market expectation. Titles priced significantly above ${yearly[2019]['avg_price']:.0f} face headwinds unless supported by strong brand or utility positioning.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cultivate perennial backlist titles.</strong> Multiple titles (e.g., classic children's books, self-help staples) appear on the bestseller list year after year with ratings above 4.7. <em>Recommendation:</em> Identify and promote backlist titles with enduring appeal — they deliver consistent revenue with minimal incremental marketing cost.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Target the 4.6–4.8 rating sweet spot.</strong> The vast majority of sustained bestsellers cluster in this range. Ratings below 4.3 rarely repeat on the list. <em>Recommendation:</em> Use early reader feedback and pre-launch reviews to gauge whether a title will clear the ~4.5 threshold before committing to large print runs or marketing spend.
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

</div>

<div class="footnote">
  Data: Amazon Top 50 Bestsellers 2009–2019 ({total} records, {unique_books} unique titles) &bull; All figures computed directly from source data &bull; No values fabricated
</div>

<script>
Chart.defaults.font.family = "'Segoe UI', -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, sans-serif";
Chart.defaults.font.size = 12;
Chart.defaults.plugins.legend.labels.usePointStyle = true;

const ficColor = '#3182ce';
const nfColor = '#e53e3e';
const priceColor = '#2c5282';
const ratingColor = '#e53e3e';
const reviewColor = '#38a169';

// Genre Split Donut
new Chart(document.getElementById('genreSplitChart'), {{
  type: 'doughnut',
  data: {{
    labels: ['Fiction ({fiction_count})', 'Non-Fiction ({nonfiction_count})'],
    datasets: [{{ data: [{fiction_count}, {nonfiction_count}], backgroundColor: [ficColor, nfColor], borderWidth: 2, borderColor: '#fff' }}]
  }},
  options: {{
    responsive: true, maintainAspectRatio: true,
    plugins: {{ legend: {{ position: 'bottom' }} }}
  }}
}});

// Scatter
new Chart(document.getElementById('scatterChart'), {{
  type: 'scatter',
  data: {{
    datasets: [
      {{ label: 'Fiction', data: {[{'x': p, 'y': r} for p, r in scatter_fiction]}, backgroundColor: ficColor + '88', borderColor: ficColor, pointRadius: 4 }},
      {{ label: 'Non-Fiction', data: {[{'x': p, 'y': r} for p, r in scatter_nonfiction]}, backgroundColor: nfColor + '88', borderColor: nfColor, pointRadius: 4 }}
    ]
  }},
  options: {{
    responsive: true, maintainAspectRatio: true,
    scales: {{
      x: {{ title: {{ display: true, text: 'Price ($)' }} }},
      y: {{ title: {{ display: true, text: 'User Rating' }}, min: 3.0, max: 5.0 }}
    }},
    plugins: {{
      legend: {{ position: 'top' }},
      title: {{ display: true, text: 'Pearson r = {pearson_r:.4f} (overall)', font: {{ size: 13 }} }}
    }}
  }}
}});

// Segment Chart (bar + line)
new Chart(document.getElementById('segmentChart'), {{
  type: 'bar',
  data: {{
    labels: {seg_labels},
    datasets: [
      {{ label: 'Book Count', data: {seg_counts}, backgroundColor: priceColor + '99', yAxisID: 'y' }},
      {{ label: 'Avg Rating', data: {seg_avg_ratings}, type: 'line', borderColor: ratingColor, backgroundColor: ratingColor, yAxisID: 'y1', tension: 0.3, pointRadius: 5 }}
    ]
  }},
  options: {{
